Issue #1683 proof: key spend against its limit now renders as a real bar

SUBSTRATE

Everything below ran against a stack booted from this branch, on this
machine, with no shared or hosted service involved:

  - a throwaway Postgres (pgvector/pgvector:pg17) carrying the full
    supabase/migrations chain, plus GoTrue and PostgREST behind one nginx
    origin, all stood up by scripts/ci-supabase-stack.sh, the same script the
    CI web-e2e job uses. It was destroyed after the capture.
  - control-plane from deploy/docker/docker-compose.yml (image
    hive-control-plane:ci) against that database, healthy on /health, on a
    non-default host port so it could not touch another checkout's stack.
  - a production `next build` plus `next start` of apps/web-console FROM THIS
    BRANCH, pointed at that control-plane, driven by a real Chromium through
    Playwright.

The three fixture users came from
apps/web-console/tests/e2e/support/e2e-auth-fixtures.mjs with a run-scoped
E2E_RUN_KEY, so no shared fixture account was touched and no password
anywhere was reset or rotated. The browser signed in through the console's own
/auth/sign-in form against that throwaway GoTrue.

The four API keys were created through the real product API, not by writing
rows: POST /api/v1/accounts/current/api-keys for each key and POST
/api/v1/accounts/current/api-keys/{id}/policy for each cap, both carrying the
signed-in user's bearer. The only row written directly is the lifetime spend,
one INSERT per key into public.api_key_usage_rollups, which is the exact table
RecordUsageFinalization writes and Repository.GetLifetimeSpend reads; a real
spend figure would otherwise have required paying a provider for tokens. Every
number on screen therefore travelled the real path: rollup table, control-plane
handler, HTTP, console server component, rendered bar.

WHAT THE CONTROL-PLANE SERVED

GET /api/v1/accounts/current/api-keys, read back before the browser ran
(nickname, spend_credits, budget_limit_credits, budget kind):

  playground     720000000   null          none
  nightly-sync   360000000   10000000000   monthly
  batch-jobs     2970000000  2000000000    lifetime
  prod-gateway   1140000000  5000000000    lifetime

One US dollar is one billion credits, so that is $0.72 uncapped, $0.36 against
a $10.00 monthly cap, $2.97 against a $2.00 lifetime cap (over), and $1.14
against a $5.00 lifetime cap (partial).

SCREENSHOTS

URL for all three: http://localhost:3010/console/api-keys
No credential appears in that URL, in any capture, or in this log. There is no
address bar in the captures.

01-api-keys-usage-bars.png
  The whole page after signing in. Four rows, four states of the same column.

02-usage-bars-closeup.png
  The table alone, which is where the change is legible:
    - prod-gateway: "$1.14 of $5.00 total", "22.8%", and a bar filled roughly
      a fifth of its track in the accent colour.
    - batch-jobs:   "$2.97 of $2.00 total", a danger-toned "Limit reached"
      badge, "148.5%", and a red bar that stops exactly at the end of its
      track rather than overflowing the column.
    - nightly-sync: "$0.36 of $10.00/mo" and no bar, deliberately. The spend
      figure is lifetime and the cap is monthly, so a ratio between them would
      be a number the enforcement path never computes.
    - playground:   "$0.72 of Unlimited" and no bar, because there is no
      denominator.

03-usage-bars-dark.png
  The same table with the dark colour scheme emulated, confirming the track,
  the accent fill and the danger fill all still read against the dark surface.

WHAT THE PROGRESS ELEMENTS ACTUALLY CARRY

Read out of the live DOM in the same browser session, in row order:

  batch-jobs    value="100"  max="100"
                aria-label="Budget used: $2.97 of $2.00 total"
                aria-valuetext="148.5%, limit reached"
  prod-gateway  value="22.8" max="100"
                aria-label="Budget used: $1.14 of $5.00 total"
                aria-valuetext="22.8%"

Two of them, one per lifetime-capped key, and none for the monthly or the
uncapped row. They are native <progress> elements, so the progressbar role,
the value and the maximum are the element's own; the accessible name carries
both dollar figures, and aria-valuetext exists so a screen reader hears
"148.5%" for the over-limit key rather than the "100%" its clamped fill would
otherwise announce.

Full row text as rendered, tabs and newlines flattened:

  playground   | hk_xxxx(redacted)  Active | $0.72 of Unlimited
  nightly-sync | hk_xxxx(redacted)  Active | $0.36 of $10.00/mo
  batch-jobs   | hk_xxxx(redacted)  Active | $2.97 of $2.00 total
                 Limit reached 148.5%
  prod-gateway | hk_xxxx(redacted)  Active | $1.14 of $5.00 total 22.8%

The key suffixes the product itself redacts down to four characters are shown
as (redacted) here as well. They belonged to keys in a database that no longer
exists.

BROWSER CONSOLE

No console errors or warnings were emitted during the capture.
